# oneAPI V‑Ray Compiler Notes

## What I was trying to do
Build a compiler I read about that can give around a 15% boost to projects by tuning everything to my setup. I wanted to understand how oneAPI, oneTBB, oneDNN, and LLVM fit together to make that happen.

## What I started with
- oneAPI base components
- oneTBB (task scheduling)
- oneDNN (optimization + learning)
- LLVM (compiler backend for tuning)

## What each part does
- **oneTBB** — Handles how tasks and data move around. Its job is to balance loads, just like you would do in any real‑world situation.
- **oneDNN** — Does the “brain work.” It optimizes tasks and learns as it goes, making everything run smoother — like tuning an engine.
- **LLVM** — Compiles oneTBB and oneDNN so they’re tuned to your specific hardware and setup.

## What happened
- I built the basic components.
- I saw how oneTBB and oneDNN act as the foundation for almost anything in the Intel compute stack.
- LLVM can recompile them so they match your system better.
- After tuning, you can rerun the process to optimize again using the tuned versions.

## What confused me
- The relationship between oneAPI, oneTBB, oneDNN, and LLVM isn’t obvious at first.
- The tuning loop makes sense once you see it: build → tune → rebuild → optimize.

## What I want to try next
- Build a full pipeline using the tuned versions.
- Test performance differences before and after tuning.
- See how this fits into bigger projects like V‑Ray or compute workloads.

